## Sentient connects coding-agent actions with local system records

Source: Hacker News · epynonymous — https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50409020
Published: 2026-11-07
Channel: hn

Sentient is an open-source monitor for coding agents that keeps activity records on the user’s machine. In a first-party Show HN announcement, its creator describes building it after noticing that repeated permission approvals had become routine. The concern was visibility into the many commands running beneath a coding session, beyond what the agent itself reported.

First, the Linux monitoring path uses Tetragon, a tool for collecting events from the operating-system kernel, to observe file, process and network activity. Agent session logs then supply context about the prompt and the agent’s stated reasoning. According to the product page, deterministic rules flag risky operations, such as changes outside the repository, for review in a local dashboard. Reviewers can inspect a change alongside its command and prompt.

The creator describes Sentient as read-only and local, with no cloud synchronization. It currently supports Claude Code; a mode using agent logs without kernel records is also available. Support for other agents and operating systems is planned. These are the developer’s claims, without independent evidence of complete capture or tamper resistance.

Why it matters: For engineers supervising local coding agents: compare system activity with session logs, and test the coverage of each collection mode before relying on the combined record.

